  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\n(Saturday Night - Tuesday)\n\nHeat/humidity is expected to expand eastward across the remainder of \nthe area on Sunday, with max afternoon heat index readings 105+ for \nmost locations both Sunday and Monday. There still is not too much \nof a signal for a thunderstorm cluster to impact the region during \nthis time frame in deterministic guidance, though the pattern \ndoes suggest it is possible. The heart of the mid/upper level \nanticyclone stays well to our west, and forward-propagating \nCorfidi vectors suggest any complex to our north very well may \nmove south into our region. That appears to be an unlikely \npossibility, but something that not only could prevent advisory \ncriteria HI values on a given day, but would also pose a threat\nfor damaging winds.\n\nThere is a bit better of a signal for some type of cold frontal \npassage late Monday night - Tuesday, though the timing varies some \n12+ hours amongst model guidance. In addition, there are some \ndiscrepancies on the air mass behind this cold front. Regardless,\nit does not look like a significant cool down, but could \nprovide enough relief from the heat to get back below advisory \ncriteria. Therefore, elected to have the heat advisory go only \nthrough Monday for now, with the possibility it may need to be \nextended in time for portions of the area.\n\n\n(Tuesday Night - Next Friday)\n\nEven slower model guidance has the cold front through the entire\narea by Tuesday night into Wednesday. Again, there are no signs\nof a significant cool down behind this front, but enough to \nallow the bi-state area to get back closer to normal \ntemperatures for late July. Mostly dry weather is also expected \nto continue, though we will have to keep an eye on at least the \npossibility of ridge-running shortwaves as the mid/upper level \nanticyclone shifts more toward the desert southwest.",
